    passage: "The debate over whether the Maya civilization collapsed has been reshaped by recent research. The traditional narrative described a sudden 'collapse' around 900 CE, when the great lowland cities of the southern Maya region were abandoned. Climate scientists have since shown that severe, multi-decade droughts struck the region during this period, and tree-ring and lake-sediment data link the timing of the worst droughts to the abandonment of major centers. But archaeologists increasingly argue that 'collapse' is a misleading term. The Maya population did not disappear; large communities persisted in the northern Yucatán for centuries, and millions of Maya people live in the region today. What happened, researchers suggest, was a political collapse—the breakdown of divine kingship and the large urban centers that depended on it—rather than a demographic or cultural one. Drought may have undermined the authority of rulers who claimed to control the rain, triggering political reorganization rather than extinction.",
