    passage: "The concept of affordances, introduced by psychologist James J. Gibson, describes the possible actions that an environment or object offers to an organism. A flat, sturdy surface affords standing; a handle affords grasping; a gap in a hedge affords passage. Crucially, affordances are relational: they exist in the interaction between the properties of the environment and the capabilities of the organism. A staircase affords climbing to a human but not to a snake, and the same object—say, a fallen log—affords different actions to a squirrel (a bridge or a perch) than to a human (a seat or an obstacle). In design, the concept has been popularized by Don Norman, who uses it to explain why some products are intuitive and others confusing: good design makes the relevant affordances visible, while poor design hides them or suggests actions that are not actually possible.",
