    passage: "The Ottoman practice of devshirme, which operated from the fourteenth to the seventeenth centuries, involved the periodic conscription of young Christian boys from the empire's Balkan territories. The boys were converted to Islam, educated in the palace schools, and trained for service in the Ottoman administration or the elite Janissary infantry corps. Viewed strictly as a system of forced recruitment, the devshirme appears brutal, and many families sought to hide their sons from the recruiters. Yet some historians emphasize that the system also created a path of upward mobility: boys from peasant villages could rise to the highest offices of the empire, and several grand viziers—the empire's chief ministers—began their careers as devshirme conscripts. The system's dual character—simultaneously an imposition of state power and a channel of advancement—makes it difficult to assess in simple moral terms.",
