    module: 2,
    domain: "Information and Ideas",
    passage: "The placebo effect—improvement in a patient's condition that follows a treatment with no active ingredient—was long regarded as a nuisance in medical research, something to be controlled for in drug trials. In recent decades, however, the placebo effect has itself become a subject of study. Researchers have found that placebos produce measurable physiological changes: patients given sugar pills for pain show reduced activity in pain-processing regions of the brain, and sham surgeries have relieved symptoms in trials for knee osteoarthritis and heart conditions. The effect appears to be mediated by expectations—the brain's anticipation of relief triggers the release of endorphins, the body's natural painkillers—and it can be enhanced by the manner of the clinician: warm, confident doctors elicit stronger placebo responses than detached ones. Yet the placebo effect has limits: it cannot shrink tumors or cure infections, and it does not work in everyone. Understanding it, researchers argue, is not about endorsing deception but about harnessing the therapeutic power of expectation honestly, for example through the quality of the doctor-patient relationship.",
