    passage: "The history of the transcontinental railroad in the United States is often told as a story of heroic engineering, and the completion of the first line in 1869—when the Union Pacific and Central Pacific rails met at Promontory Summit, Utah—was indeed a remarkable feat. But recent scholarship has emphasized a darker dimension of the story. The Central Pacific's workforce was overwhelmingly Chinese: at the peak of construction, roughly 80 percent of its laborers were immigrants from Guangdong province, who performed the most dangerous tasks, including blasting tunnels through the Sierra Nevada. They were paid less than their white counterparts and were excluded from the celebratory photographs taken at Promontory Summit. Moreover, the railroad was built with substantial government subsidies—land grants and loans—that enriched a small number of financiers, several of whom were later implicated in the Credit Mobilier scandal, in which construction contracts were inflated to siphon profits. The transcontinental railroad transformed the American economy, but the standard celebratory narrative, scholars argue, obscures the exploitation and corruption that accompanied its construction.",
